Interaction between fluids and Helfrich membranes
Dennis W ̈orthmuller,1, 2 Pierre Sens,1, 2 and Michele Castellana1, 2, ∗
1Institut Curie, PSL Research University, Paris, France
2CNRS UMR168, 11 rue Pierre et Marie Curie, 75005, Paris, France
(Dated: November 6, 2026)
We propose afinite elementmethod to determine the dynamics of a fluid interacting with a membrane—a fluid layer equipped with a Helfrich elastic free energy [W. Helfrich,Z. F ̈ur Naturforschung C, 28, 693, (1973)]. Our analysis is based on a partitioned approach, where the state of the fluid, of the membrane, and of the mesh, are solved for at staggered steps. We show the predicting capabilities of the method by considering a macroscopic example of of an elastomer membrane, which bulges as it interacts with air injected through a channel. In particular, we demonstrate that our method allows for describing large membrane deformations which are not accessible by perturbative methods. We discuss the limitations of our method, by characterizing its added-mass instability. Finally, we show how our analysis allows for understanding the physical mechanism of bulge formation, which is due to a combination of elastic stresses and membrane boundary conditions.
I. INTRODUCTION
A. State of the art
A number of existing studies rely on the hypothesis that thefluidsurrounding theHelfrich membraneis inertialess. For instance, Arroyo et al. study the interaction between aHelfrich membraneand bulk fluid in the Stokes approximation, for simple membrane geometries that can be described by a single parameter [1]. Boedec and coauthors combined FE andboundary element( BE) methods describe anHelfrich membraneinteracting with an inertialessfluid[ 2]. In there, the membrane surface rheology, such as viscous effects and flows induced by surface-tension gradients, is neglected. AHelfrich membranecoupled to afluidhas been recently studied by Zhou and co-authors with BE and FE methods, under the assumption that thefluidis inertialess and thatHelfrich membraneis inextensible [ 3]. Barakat and Shaqfeh modelled vesicles in a circular tube by means of BE methods and lubrication theory, by neglectingHelfrich membrane rheology and describingfluidas inertialess [ 4]. Finally, Rodrigues et al., studied the interaction between an inertialess, closedHelfrich membranes andfluidswith FE methods, by adopting a minimal description for the fluid, which is solely modelled as a volume constraint [5].
Other studies go beyond the inertialess approximation, and rely on simplifying assumptions for theHelfrich membrane. For instance, Bonito et al. propose a FE model for aHelfrich membraneinteracting anfluid, the latter being in the inertial regime [6, 7]. In this
