1.Helfrich membranemotion
Both the physical nature and mathematical description of M is significantly more complex than that of E. First, unlike E, M has a fluid structure: Its material elements can flow tangentially to the M shape, which also moves subjected to bending-rigidity forces, and to the force exerted on it by F. While the tangential motion is naturally suited to an Eulerian description [19], the shape motion—reminiscent of that of an elastic body—is best described with a Lagrangian approach [30]. Also, such shape motion needs to be connected to the Lagrangian approach used to describe D. Second, M is described by fourth-orderpartial differential equations( PDEs) [31]. From the mathematical and numerical point of view, such PDEs are significantly more complex than the secondorder PDEs which describe E [32]. On top of this, from the physical standpoint, such fourth-order PDEs require moreboundary conditions( BCs) than the second-order ones for E: Such BCs may specify a number of physical features of the M system, such as forces, positions, torques, or stresses. Third, the geometrical description of M requires fixing an appropriate gauge. In fact, a general parametrization of M is given by two functions—the two Cartesian components of xM
