III. DISCUSSION
We developed a numerical study on the interaction between afluid( F) and aHelfrich membrane( M). Our analysis is based on thefinite element( FE) method, and it leverages theFinite Element Computational Software( FEniCS) [13], combined with thefluId layeR finitEElement softwarE( IRENE) [32]. In order to guide and introduce the reader to the interaction problem between afluidandHelfrich membrane, we provided two introductory examples of fluid-structure interaction [16], in which thefluidinteracts with arigid bodyand with anelastic body, see SectionsII AandII B. These Sections allow to introduce the necessary physical an mathematical quantities, as well as to assess the stability of our numerical scheme.
In particular, the time-iteration scheme that we propose implies a partitioned resolution scheme [45] well known influid-structure interaction( FSI) problems. In this regard, we denote by B, E or M the ‘structure’ with which F interacts, respectively, in SectionsII AtoII C. In such partitioned scheme one solves—for example—for the structure degrees of freedom in a given, fixed configuration of F andFluid domain( D), and similarly for F and D, see for example Cases1to3in SectionII C 4.
Partitioned solutions are known to suffer for numerical instabilities [46]. Qualitatively speaking, as the structure movement displaces F elements, for the structure to accelerate with an acceleration a, an effective ‘added’ F mass, mA, needs to be displaced. This mass results into an effective force ∼ a mA that F exerts on the structure. The partitioned scheme turns out to be stable if such force is significantly smaller than the structure inertia, a mS [47], i.e., if
