First, for the motion of B in F of SectionII A, mA ∼ ρFAS ∼ 2.3 × 10−2 kg, and mS = ρBAS = 1.4 × 10−1 kg,
where AS = πab is the area of B. The stability condition (127) is thus satisfied with an accuracy of mA/mS ∼ 10−2. Second, in the analysis of SectionII B, where E moves in F, mA ∼ ρFAS ∼ 6.2×10−2 kg, and mS = ρEAS = 6.2×101 kg, where AS ∼ π(ab − r2) is the area of E. The stability condition (127) is thus satisfied with an accuracy of mA/mS ∼ 0.1.
Third, we discuss the added-mass instability for the interaction between F and M of SectionII C. At the time shown in Fig.4, we have mS ∼ ρMl ∼ 1 kg, mA ∼ ρFl2 ∼ 1 kg, where l ∼ 1 m is the average M length in thecurrentconfiguration. As a result, Eq. (127) is border line; added and structure mass are comparable, mA/mS ∼ 1. More importantly, theboundary conditions used in SectionII C 1allow for M elements to freely flow in through theleftextremity: as a result, l grows in time. Given that mS and mA scale with l as ∼ l and ∼ l2, respectively, the longer the system evolves, the more the condition (127) will be violated.
We will now show how our model allows for understanding the physics of the bulge formation in Fig.4. The BCs (34) to (41) are such that the L extremity of M, ∂Ωr , is pinned, but its tangent angle ψ is free. As a result, M tilts upwards under the force exerted by F in the vicinity of ∂Ωr , taking advantage of the free angle. The M shape thus bends in the vicinity of the symmetry axis ∂Ωr , accumulating an elastic stress. The normal acceleration ∂wM/∂t, which is referred to the outwards direction of the current normal nˆc shown in Fig.3, receives contributions from multiple terms, see Eq. (22). These are the forces, which appear in theright-hand sideof Eq. (22), and inertial terms in theleft-hand side. First, the forces are the Laplace force fL, Eq. (26), the elastic force fκ, Eq. (27), the viscous force due to the flow of M elements
fη
M n [35], Eq. (29), and the force fF n exerted by F on M, Eq. (19). Second, the inertial terms Iv, Iw are given in (32) and (33). Figure6depicts all such terms in an early stage of the bulge formation, showing that the elastic force fκ dominates over all terms. Such force releases the accumulated elastic stress above: It pushes M inwards in the vicinity of ∂Ωr and outwards close to ∂Ωr —see Fig.6B. Given that M is pinned at ∂Ωr , the force at ∂Ωr is neutralized by an equal and opposite force, cf. Eq. (39). On the other hand, at ∂Ωr , M is free to move upwards driven by the elastic force, cf. Eq. (40), resulting into an increase of the M bulge. As a result, the inflation mechanism is due to a combination of the free-angle BC and elastic forces.
As a future direction of this work, we plan to go beyond the partitioned approach by using a monolithic scheme, where the degrees of freedom of the M, F and D are solved for at the same time [48]. For a monolithic scheme, no added-mass instability exists, allowing for a characterization of a broader range of physical regimes, e.g., deformation even larger than that of Fig.4. This would allow for studying a large variety of physically sensible


